            <div xml:id="d409">

                <pb facs="0418" n="406" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg406"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War, 1939/16: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-01T20:00:00+01:00">September 1,
                            1939—8 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 1—2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1365. My 1358, September 1, 5 p.m., last paragraph. The terms of the second note
                    to Germany will depend, of course, on the nature of the German reply to the note
                    already despatched and the Government has under consideration several
                    alternatives. They naturally have in mind that Germany may make no reply at all,
                    which is not improbable.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
